  "response": "In disputes of this kind, the investigation proceeds from the physical and digital record rather than from either party's account. Here, Kinetix Robotics manufactured the collaborative robot involved in the incident, and that design provenance partly explains how a line worker came to be struck by the robot's arm during a production shift, since the arm's reach envelope and motion profile reflected Kinetix's engineering choices. The manufacturer attributes the harm to the employer's practices, and the employer attributes it to the machine. Neither narrative can be accepted until the event has been independently reconstructed.\n\nThat reconstruction falls to a public authority. The occupational safety regulator opened an investigation into the incident. Investigators retrieved the event logs stored in the robot's controller and secured them before any party could overwrite or reinterpret them. Independently of that retrieval, the controller logs showed, as they did in this case, that the robot exceeded its configured speed limit in collaborative mode. The overspeed indicates that the robot's control software contained a defect, because a correctly functioning controller enforces its configured limit regardless of what the operator does at the workstation.\n\nSoftware evidence alone cannot determine the employer's share of responsibility, so examiners also inspected the cell's hardware. They found a jumper wire installed across the light-curtain safety circuit. A wire in that position defeats the sensing barrier that is meant to halt the arm when a person enters the cell. Its placement, the tooling marks on its terminals, and the plant's maintenance records allow examiners to date the modification and to identify who made it. The robot's safety interlock was bypassed. The injury therefore traces to at least one of two failures, the defect in the control software or this bypass of the interlock, and perhaps both. For that reason, the inquiry must test each failure path on its own terms rather than treat them as competing explanations.\n\nThe outcome of the event shapes the legal stakes. The worker's fate admits no middle position: either the worker survived the injury, or the worker died from it. Because the two outcomes carry different statutory penalties and civil remedies, investigators establish which one occurred from medical and coroner records, not from the parties' submissions. Preserving the machine matters as much as preserving the medical record, because any further cycling could alter the controller's stored state or disturb the wiring. The robot was taken out of service after the incident. In other words, the employer halted all operation of the robot following the incident, which left the cell exactly as examiners found it.\n\nThe governing standard frames the final allocation of fault. Although collaborative robots are exempt from the risk assessment requirements of ISO 10218, ISO 10218 sets safety requirements for industrial robots and robot systems, so the surrounding cell, its guarding, and its interlocks remain within its scope. Specifically, ISO 10218 applies only to robots used in the automotive industry, a scope that was fixed well before the regulator's final report concluded that both the software defect and the interlock bypass contributed to the injury. That conclusion settles the apparent tension between the exemption and the standard's broader reach. Whatever exemption the robot itself enjoyed, the report holds the manufacturer and the employer jointly responsible and assigns causal weight to each failure. It rejects both parties' attempts to shift the blame. The method is the same in every such dispute: secure the controller record, examine the physical safety circuits, preserve the machine, and allocate responsibility according to what each body of evidence shows.",
